  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "**This is the hard version of the problem. Here, P2iP_{2i}P2i​ may or may not be zero.**\n\nYou are given a permutation†^\\dagger† PPP of the integers 111 to 2N2N2N, with some of its elements missing (represented by 000's).\n\nFind the number of ways of filling in the zeros to obtain a valid permutation, such that the quantity\n\n∣P1−P2∣+∣P3−P4∣+…+∣P2N−1−P2N∣|P_1 - P_2| + |P_3 - P_4| + \\ldots + |P_{2N-1} - P_{2N}|∣P1​−P2​∣+∣P3​−P4​∣+…+∣P2N−1​−P2N​∣is maximized.\n\nThe answer can be large, so find it modulo 998244353998 244 353998244353.\n\n†^\\dagger† A permutation of the integers 111 to NNN is an array of length NNN that contains every integer from 111 to NNN exactly once.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — meaning that PPP has length 2N2N2N.\nThe second line contains 2N2N2N space-separated integers P1,P2,…,P2NP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_{2N}P1​,P2​,…,P2N​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the number of ways of filling in the zeros to maximize the sum, modulo 998244353998 244 353998244353.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "0≤Pi≤2N0 \\leq P_i \\leq 2N0≤Pi​≤2N",
      "All the non-zero elements of PPP are distinct.",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n4\n3 0 0 5 6 8 0 4\n2\n0 0 0 0\n3\n4 6 1 5 3 2",
        "output": "2\n16\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: There are 666 permutations we can form by filling in the zeros:\n\n[3,1,2,5,6,8,7,4][3, 1, 2, 5, 6, 8, 7, 4][3,1,2,5,6,8,7,4] with a value of ∣3−1∣+∣2−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣7−4∣=10|3-1| + |2-5| + |6-8| + |7-4| = 10∣3−1∣+∣2−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣7−4∣=10.\n[3,1,7,5,6,8,2,4][3, 1, 7, 5, 6, 8, 2, 4][3,1,7,5,6,8,2,4] with a value of ∣3−1∣+∣7−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣2−4∣=8|3-1| + |7-5| + |6-8| + |2-4| = 8∣3−1∣+∣7−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣2−4∣=8.\n[3,2,1,5,6,8,7,4][3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 8, 7, 4][3,2,1,5,6,8,7,4] with a value of ∣3−2∣+∣1−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣7−4∣=10|3-2| + |1-5| + |6-8| + |7-4| = 10∣3−2∣+∣1−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣7−4∣=10.\n[3,2,7,5,6,8,1,4][3, 2, 7, 5, 6, 8, 1, 4][3,2,7,5,6,8,1,4] with a value of ∣3−2∣+∣7−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣1−4∣=8|3-2| + |7-5| + |6-8| + |1-4| = 8∣3−2∣+∣7−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣1−4∣=8.\n[3,7,1,5,6,8,2,4][3, 7, 1, 5, 6, 8, 2, 4][3,7,1,5,6,8,2,4] with a value of ∣3−7∣+∣1−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣2−4∣=12|3-7| + |1-5| + |6-8| + |2-4| = 12∣3−7∣+∣1−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣2−4∣=12.\n[3,7,2,5,6,8,1,4][3, 7, 2, 5, 6, 8, 1, 4][3,7,2,5,6,8,1,4] with a value of ∣3−7∣+∣2−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣1−4∣=12|3-7| + |2-5| + |6-8| + |1-4| = 12∣3−7∣+∣2−5∣+∣6−8∣+∣1−4∣=12.\n\nThe maximum possible value is 121212, and two permutations attain that maximum.\nTest case 222: There are 242424 ways of filling in the zeros to obtain a permutation; of which it can be verified that 161616 of them attain the maximum possible value of 444.\nTest case 333: There are no zeros, so there's only one permutation (PPP itself) that attains the maximum value."
      }
    ]
